// Tour - Code for one target.
//
// A line that starts with `#` is a directive. `#target` goes at the top of a
// file and says which targets the file is for; anywhere else the file's
// declarations simply do not exist. The words are architectures (`x64`,
// `lua`), systems (`linux`) or target names (`linux_x64`, `cc`), joined with
// `&&`, `||`, `!` and parentheses: `#target x64 && linux`, or
// `#target (x64 || lua) && !windows`.
//
// This folder is a package. A file in it with a `#target` is a variant of this
// entry: when its target is the one being built it shares this file's names,
// private ones included, and when it is not it contributes nothing. x64.wsp and
// lua.wsp both declare `width`, and only one of them exists in a build. A file
// without a directive is a part of the package, as in the geometry folder.
//
// `native<...>` takes the same expressions. When several versions of a
// function match, the one that matches fewer targets wins: `native<x64 &&
// linux>` beats `native<x64>`.

import "std/fmt";

public func describe() string {
  return name();
}

public func demo() {
  fmt.printf("this build is for the {} architecture, {} bits wide\n", name(), width());
}
